WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.041
Our backend team is small, so I lead
it by directing AI coding agents.

2
00:00:06.441 --> 00:00:11.161
I set the goal and the rules for each
piece of work, and ask for a plan.

3
00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:15.645
Agents write much of the code, the
load-test tools and the reports.

4
00:00:15.895 --> 00:00:19.745
I make the go and no-go calls,
and review what comes back.

5
00:00:19.995 --> 00:00:25.121
Design decisions get written down, and
there are 60 decision records so far.

6
00:00:25.371 --> 00:00:32.597
Of my 2,782 backend commits, 1,503
were co-written with an agent.

7
00:00:33.097 --> 00:00:35.491
That only works if
checking the work is fast.

8
00:00:35.741 --> 00:00:37.741
Our test suite took about 30 minutes,

9
00:00:37.741 --> 00:00:41.476
and every pull request paid
for it two or three times.

10
00:00:41.726 --> 00:00:43.045
The tests weren't slow.

11
00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:44.585
The setup was,

12
00:00:44.585 --> 00:00:49.485
with mocks rebuilt for every test and real
password hashing for every fake user.

13
00:00:49.735 --> 00:00:53.845
I made the setup cheap and spread
the suite across 24 workers.

14
00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:58.748
Now 8,253 tests run in 91 seconds.

15
00:00:59.248 --> 00:01:02.442
One workflow now deploys
all three environments.

16
00:01:02.692 --> 00:01:08.045
In its first two weeks, a production deploy
took a median of 2 minutes 40 seconds.
